- **About 70%** of online shopping carts are abandoned before purchase, the average of 50 studies (Source: [Baymard Institute](https://baymard.com/lists/cart-abandonment-rate))
- **270%** greater purchase likelihood for a product with five reviews than one with none (Source: [Spiegel Research Center](https://spiegel.medill.northwestern.edu/how-online-reviews-influence-sales/))

## Why visitors don't buy

### The Trust Gap

People are scared of being scammed. If your site looks generic or lacks social proof, they bounce, even if your product is great.

### Friction

If your site is slow, navigation is confusing, or checkout takes too many steps, they leave. Every extra click is a reason to quit.

### Messaging

If the benefit of your product isn't clear within three seconds, you have lost them. Benefits beat features every time.

## First, find where buyers drop off

Before changing anything, measure where people leave. Break the journey into steps and count how many visitors reach each one:

1. **Product page:** visitors who view a product.
2. **Cart:** visitors who add something to the cart.
3. **Checkout:** visitors who start checkout.
4. **Purchase:** visitors who complete payment.

The biggest drop between two steps is where to start. A store that loses most people on the product page has a messaging or trust problem. A store that loses them at checkout has a friction problem. Fixing the wrong step wastes weeks, so let the numbers choose the order of the fixes below.

## The 4 fixes that move the needle

01

### Fix your product page copy

Stop listing features and start listing benefits. People do not buy a mattress because it has 400 coils. They buy it because they want to stop waking up with back pain. Your copy should speak to the transformation your product provides.

In practice, rewrite the first two lines under the product title to answer three questions: who is this for, what problem does it solve, and what does life look like after buying it. Move technical specifications lower down, into a section for the people who want them. Use the words your customers use in reviews and support messages, not internal product language.

02

### Move your social proof

Most stores hide their reviews at the very bottom of the page. You should have star ratings right under the product title. You should have 'Featured in' logos near the Add to Cart button. Social proof should be visible at every stage of the journey.

Products with reviews outperform products without them, so ask every customer for one after delivery and make it easy: one tap to rate, with an optional line of text. Show reviews that mention specific outcomes, add photos from real customers where you have them, and repeat the strongest proof near the checkout button, where doubt is highest.

03

### Remove checkout friction

If you require people to create an account before they can buy, you are killing your conversion rate. Offer guest checkout. Offer one-click payment options like Apple Pay or UPI. Every field you remove from your checkout form will increase your revenue.

Walk through your own checkout on a phone and count the steps. Show delivery costs and dates before the final screen, because unexpected costs at the last step are a common reason people abandon. Auto-fill addresses where you can, keep the cart summary visible, and make sure error messages say exactly which field needs fixing.

04

### Build real abandoned cart sequences

Most brands send one boring email that says 'You forgot something.' That is not enough. You should have a 3–4 part sequence: a reminder after one hour, a testimonial after 24 hours, a limited time discount or FAQ after 48 hours.

Write each email for a different reason someone hesitated: the first simply reminds, the second answers doubts with reviews or common questions, and the last gives a reason to act now. Show the actual product they left behind, link straight back to a filled cart, and stop the sequence the moment they buy.

## Test one change at a time

Change one thing, measure it for long enough to see a real difference, then move on. If you rewrite the product page, move the reviews and shorten checkout in the same week, you will not know which change worked, or which one quietly hurt. Keep a simple log of each change, the date and the funnel step it targeted, and compare that step's numbers before and after.

## For high-ticket B2B eCommerce: add outbound

For high-ticket B2B eCommerce, you can even use outbound. If you see a major account visiting your site repeatedly, you do not wait for them to fill out a form. You use your outbound engine to reach out to the decision makers at that company with a helpful, context-led message.

The same thinking works for eCommerce technology companies selling to stores. In one engagement, cold email for an eCommerce SaaS company booked 28 meetings with a 41% open rate and generated $400K in pipeline in six weeks. [Read the case study](/case-studies/ecommerce-conversion-playbook).

## The bottom line

Too many founders look for a 'hack' or a 'trick' to increase conversion. Real conversion is about building a system that respects the user and provides genuine value at every step. Conversion is not about tricking people into buying. It is about making it as easy as possible for the right people to say yes.
